<h2>What we build</h2>

<p><strong>Wainscoting.</strong> Panels or boards on the lower part of the wall, finished with a cap rail. It protects walls in busy spaces like hallways, stairways and dining rooms, and it adds a traditional look that suits older homes.</p>

<p><strong>Board and batten and picture-frame moulding.</strong> Vertical battens over a flat panel, or moulding laid out in boxes, either partway up the wall or floor to ceiling. Spacing is worked out for each wall so the boxes or battens are even from end to end and line up with windows, outlets and corners.</p>

<p><strong>Feature walls.</strong> A single wall treated as a focal point, behind a bed, a sofa or a desk, or at the end of a hallway. Slats, panels and trim can all work, and paint colour plays a big part. One client described what we did in her upstairs hallway: "he added frames and lighting combined with dark paint, giving the house a very elegant look."</p>

<p><strong>Fireplace surrounds and mantels.</strong> The fireplace wall is often the room's main feature. In Perth we built a multi-layered mantel with decorative corbels, an arched header and dentil crown moulding, with a large-format <a href="{{ url('/services/tile-installation') }}">tile</a> surround and a raised step hearth. In a Carleton Place living room we built out a drywall surround with a recessed niche, a wood sill and baseboard wrapped all the way around, below a floating mantel shelf. Fireplace work is planned around the required clearances from combustible materials and the appliance manufacturer's instructions. Gas work, such as connecting a gas fireplace, isn't included.</p>

<p><strong>Built-in walls.</strong> Sometimes a feature wall is also storage. In a Kanata living room we built a full-wall built-in with open shelving, closed cabinets with panelled doors and crown moulding, all in white, tying in with the white mantel on the next wall (see <a href="{{ url('/services/custom-built-ins-closets') }}">custom built-ins</a>).</p>

<h2>Layout comes first</h2>

<p>Walls are rarely perfectly flat or square, and ceilings and floors aren't always level. We measure each wall, plan the spacing on paper, then mark it out with a laser level so the panels, battens and boxes come out even and straight. We find studs or add backing where trim or a mantel needs solid fastening, scribe to uneven walls, and fill and caulk so everything is ready for paint.</p>

<h2>Lighting and outlets</h2>

<p>Feature walls often include sconces, picture lights, a TV outlet or an outlet in a niche. Any wiring is done by qualified, certified electricians, whom we arrange as part of the project, so you still have one point of contact.</p>

<h2>Residential and commercial</h2>

<p>Most of our trim work is in homes, but we also do feature walls and finish carpentry in condos and commercial spaces such as offices and shops.</p>

<h2>How an accent wall job works</h2>

<p>You send photos of the wall and any inspiration pictures. We visit to measure and check the wall. Then you get a written estimate in Canadian dollars, labour-only or with materials, along with a schedule. We build, fill and caulk so the wall is ready for paint, and we'll agree on painting as part of the quote. Workmanship is covered by our one-year limited <a href="{{ url('/warranty') }}">warranty</a>.</p>

<h2>Why Zanini Renovations for accent walls</h2>

<p>Accent walls are all about straight lines and even spacing, and that's where hurried work shows. You deal directly with the owner, Jonas Zanini, from the estimate to the final walkthrough. Zanini Renovations is fully insured.</p>

